{
  "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
  "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Supreme Court",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:10 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Scott J. Forster",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The issue before the Court in this case is whether the discharge provision of 924(c) carries with it some requirement of intent. We believe that the answer to this question is yes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Forster, there are three levels under this: Possession, brandishing, and if the gun is discharged. You don't quarrel with the notion that Dean at least brandished this gun?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Excuse me. What -- what -- I guess I'm not following this. You say it would be true that if the blank -- if the bank guard fires his own gun when -- when the bank robber is fleeing, that would come within this?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Why? I can't get anywhere with the language, to tell you the truth. I could read it either way. It -- the House language is the same. The person \"discharges,\" yeah, but what if he discharges it accidentally? Is the accidental case or unintended case meant to be covered or not meant to be covered?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FORSTER: Your Honor, I don't think that's the -- the best way to read it. I think it has to be during and in relation to the bank robbery."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Let me ask you a question about that. I thought that \"or possesses\" was separate from the \"uses or carries.\" Is possession an example of using or carrying or is it as it says -- \"or who in furtherance of such crime possesses\"? Is that a separate -- separate enhancement?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Why do we have to find that the phrase \"if a firearm is brandished\" and the later phrase \"if a firearm is discharged\" require intentional brandishing and intentional discharging? Why can't we limit it by saying, oh, of course it means if the firearm is brandished by the bank robber or by the felon or if it's discharged by the felon, but leaving it quite undetermined whether it has to be intentionally discharged, or even intentionally brandished for that matter."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0001-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MAYNARD: If the section 924(c) offense is -- is -- is ongoing and if the firearm is discharged, the mandatory minimum sentence applies."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0001-0010",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MAYNARD: If the teller grabs the gun from the robber and it discharges, as long as the section 924(c) offense is continuing, then the firearm is discharged."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0001-0012",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MAYNARD: I think it might be a harder case for us if it were an element of the crime, but it wouldn't be an element that would be necessary."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0001-0013",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MAYNARD: I think there is a background rule with respect to the definition of criminal offenses that Congress intends some mens rea."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0001-0014",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MAYNARD: They both engaged in inherently dangerous activity."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0001-0016",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MAYNARD: I think that's correct, Your Honor, and that's our view."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0002-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It says \"if the firearm is discharged,\" and I think it's the present tense that gets you the -- the temporal connection to the crime here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But the House would have set the penalty for a discharge at 20 years. Okay. That's -- you can infer from that that the House thought an intentional discharge should subject you to 20 years."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "df54ad0cd6252e33e73d84728290acc531876ba010acd1486a863993b824b19c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-df54ad0cd625-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Is there a difference, a practical difference, between the two formulations?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2008-08-5274",
      "confidence": 0.0,
